<p>← LOOKING BACK &nbsp;|&nbsp; LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>The four thinkers before Nietzsche worked within the moral tradition he is questioning: they debated what morality requires without asking what morality is doing. Nietzsche's genealogical challenge is the hinge of this course. Unit 6 takes up Simone de Beauvoir, who accepts that there are no fixed moral essences and no eternal moral truths, but who argues that this recognition is a beginning rather than an end: it is what makes genuine ethics possible, because only beings who are free and responsible, without the comfort of a predetermined moral order, can truly choose how to live.</p>
